olution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=81\\,⟹\\,\\omega =9.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-64252/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-64252"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94277/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "bd4b472b-2119-5f0a-913a-730ef0b559df" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94277/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "9f393e14-7d5e-5c31-8560-4d7c01b4c141" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94277/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "383f3c1a-af95-566a-a265-662762442ad0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94277/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "e6a5c813-a340-57f6-a141-7f909717a6a3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94277/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "c588a69b-21ff-5b13-ba7f-61de617aca1e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94277/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "81074791-c985-5f03-9676-e010448eda45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94277/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-94277/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-94277/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-94277/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-94277/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-94277/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94277" :question/id #uuid "b5104a46-b15f-584e-909c-ecd7bde371f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-94277" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+49y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R{sin}^{2}⁡(7t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{sec}⁡7t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡7t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=49\\,⟹\\,\\omega =7.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡7t$\n\nTherefore, only function I could be used to represent  $y(t).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-94277/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94277"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94276/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "028751f5-e01b-5e60-8837-5eb3bd6543cf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94276/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "77473c6c-d74c-55ce-a380-7688429bdf58" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94276/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "c4c4a7ec-208a-5a75-8e1b-dd31909d3c74" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94276/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "9eac416e-dba2-5471-97fd-174d5f428e5c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94276/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "91d54d94-55b3-59b4-b9cb-fa4cfedfad05" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94276/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "03a730bc-ec4f-59ea-89fe-527c35606f96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94276/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-94276/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-94276/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-94276/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-94276/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-94276/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94276" :question/id #uuid "d6b1b519-5719-57bf-b52d-dec5a9c2c7f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-94276" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡16t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡16t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and II could be used to represent $y(t).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-94276/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94276"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94283/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "945c5516-71f9-523a-bc0b-88dc3521e65e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94283/selection/2edf353dc47d221855e5e7ee4be942392390ff01766e089c29dc368c2051b12d" :answer/id #uuid "57a282d2-9a93-5005-aa30-af7cf6c9da21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94283/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "67ac4f49-fbe1-5613-8190-1c7179046701" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94283/selection/af5580a233ee3709064249d6732fc822dfb93f38caf90c025deb51b127e90b2c" :answer/id #uuid "12b540b2-1b8d-51f5-8593-c4970b3980e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94283/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "b39adf3b-66de-5973-b46b-95f3653ac246" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94283/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ba5b2826-7030-5826-895d-00f809968765" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94283/selection/2edf353dc47d221855e5e7ee4be942392390ff01766e089c29dc368c2051b12d" :answer-field/choices ["q-94283/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-94283/selection/2edf353dc47d221855e5e7ee4be942392390ff01766e089c29dc368c2051b12d" "q-94283/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-94283/selection/af5580a233ee3709064249d6732fc822dfb93f38caf90c025deb51b127e90b2c" "q-94283/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94283" :question/id #uuid "3cf6d9af-b061-5993-b553-d544e56a866d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94283" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=8\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $4\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2\\sqrt{2}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n4=2\\sqrt{2}R\\,⟹\\,R=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94283/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94283"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-64253/selection/8da5fdd11b1d77b38318de764e2595cc47a2bed27fd32da66f748a2fe0c0338e" :answer/id #uuid "60acd33a-75c3-58a1-b031-34dff62a081c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64253/selection/1404f3d3729d38c0a42f770c059db3dd2fc90c02f320711801f7e6cc341847b7" :answer/id #uuid "4a95dfd5-717e-5439-a97e-19e8ae4e8ae2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64253/selection/b1474706ee02433fd7b962901874638cccd5805070af01fc2a7609d2600ffa7a" :answer/id #uuid "1925c158-9f84-5270-826c-0ad56fbc5851" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64253/selection/458e4370765c162b4c767923f78e15dcc947642c541cbe70e1696f96771d06a2" :answer/id #uuid "c650af33-cd36-5197-8a7f-797cfc76480d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64253/selection/dbbf552523e2b77610b46f6166a507d489a7f8b41418bb6a5b457c01b27a7387" :answer/id #uuid "fe9b034a-f4e4-50ff-a31a-b8115cfd73b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64253/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea1b4a19-42fd-5d9c-b6cf-9c3aef296fc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-64253/selection/1404f3d3729d38c0a42f770c059db3dd2fc90c02f320711801f7e6cc341847b7" :answer-field/choices ["q-64253/selection/8da5fdd11b1d77b38318de764e2595cc47a2bed27fd32da66f748a2fe0c0338e" "q-64253/selection/1404f3d3729d38c0a42f770c059db3dd2fc90c02f320711801f7e6cc341847b7" "q-64253/selection/b1474706ee02433fd7b962901874638cccd5805070af01fc2a7609d2600ffa7a" "q-64253/selection/458e4370765c162b4c767923f78e15dcc947642c541cbe70e1696f96771d06a2" "q-64253/selection/dbbf552523e2b77610b46f6166a507d489a7f8b41418bb6a5b457c01b27a7387"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-64253" :question/id #uuid "d81de893-b23d-5af6-ac6e-27b0b49baeee" :question/math-academy-id "q-64253" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+64y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=64\\,⟹\\,\\omega =8.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $4\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8R\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n4=8R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-64253/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-64253"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-64254"] :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+144y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $8\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=144\\,⟹\\,\\omega =12.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡12t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=8.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=12R\\operatorname{cos}⁡12t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n8=12R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡12t.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-79493/selection/39ca5713b2fbb186a9ebe6fe6b7a7184bd8ee9732981fb9e3bee1548d7b9fffd" :answer/id #uuid "b0857941-97d3-533d-adbd-03f501d0585f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79493/selection/239a7b64b769c1c3759cd853548d8c1fefc47d45b5465d1c23d73ba1c47e14a6" :answer/id #uuid "8bb97c6f-fa22-54b4-9207-eaad2a3fdc23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79493/selection/604e0a67320a59d8fbb8672d382c92fe490b634a2193108ccfdbeeecad061fd3" :answer/id #uuid "5dd28336-979b-5ffd-b4eb-ae1b39767cfc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79493/selection/dbbf552523e2b77610b46f6166a507d489a7f8b41418bb6a5b457c01b27a7387" :answer/id #uuid "74e4f416-2900-5f05-8123-58875e5c009e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79493/selection/1404f3d3729d38c0a42f770c059db3dd2fc90c02f320711801f7e6cc341847b7" :answer/id #uuid "538663fb-1e95-5331-bcba-362600490501" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79493/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d17d438e-89ca-5ae1-8f9b-aa4f60df8942" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79493/selection/dbbf552523e2b77610b46f6166a507d489a7f8b41418bb6a5b457c01b27a7387" :answer-field/choices ["q-79493/selection/39ca5713b2fbb186a9ebe6fe6b7a7184bd8ee9732981fb9e3bee1548d7b9fffd" "q-79493/selection/239a7b64b769c1c3759cd853548d8c1fefc47d45b5465d1c23d73ba1c47e14a6" "q-79493/selection/604e0a67320a59d8fbb8672d382c92fe490b634a2193108ccfdbeeecad061fd3" "q-79493/selection/dbbf552523e2b77610b46f6166a507d489a7f8b41418bb6a5b457c01b27a7387" "q-79493/selection/1404f3d3729d38c0a42f770c059db3dd2fc90c02f320711801f7e6cc341847b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79493" :question/id #uuid "eb0bc318-53b7-59c2-b2a3-48178245e046" :question/math-academy-id "q-79493" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $6\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $6\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=6.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3R\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=6$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n6=3R\\,⟹\\,R=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $2\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-79493/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79493"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94280/selection/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" :answer/id #uuid "ed47b792-bfbe-51b2-bc2f-cc4c8c2ab073" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94280/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "1de0043c-3238-5249-825f-c4e6bfde157b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94280/selection/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" :answer/id #uuid "496c7b11-f702-5012-87e0-4579a2b83b0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94280/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "e84c053d-9bcc-54ee-830f-545ad40a6c7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94280/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "a7219203-08e9-5690-8b5a-045f99fc4ff9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94280/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "39a30bbd-c3ce-590f-a1c9-0336a5525516" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94280/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer-field/choices ["q-94280/selection/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" "q-94280/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" "q-94280/selection/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" "q-94280/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-94280/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94280" :question/id #uuid "bd131a55-d383-5fe1-8032-dc517653bbff" :question/math-academy-id "q-94280" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=25\\,⟹\\,\\omega =5.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5R\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}=5R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94280/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94280"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94288/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "749cfb50-e0b2-5973-b973-f49a432481bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94288/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "03177b05-c611-5c39-b9b3-f9028491d1d0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94288/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "b8c0bfcf-9723-5eca-9ea5-e95139a819b1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94288/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "d2d9e126-9c48-553c-8a46-6c9fe84ed935" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94288/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "6214187d-fbb8-5d95-bb10-eec3670ddd81" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94288/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c0b50ba6-2aec-59c1-9d00-713d93c0c76b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94288/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-94288/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-94288/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-94288/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-94288/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-94288/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94288" :question/id #uuid "0f7fe2d4-f6a4-50af-961e-e17d97fe8283" :question/math-academy-id "q-94288" :question/problem "A particle of mass $7\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=21\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+3y=0$\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ),$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{21}{7}=3\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\sqrt{3}$ and ${ϕ}_{1}=ϕ$ in form 2 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-94288/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94288"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-64258/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "4beab6ab-a866-557a-9b7c-da9f31065be9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64258/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "9830b004-0491-5d2e-99e5-d688bb3496a1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64258/selection/18f1ea50d5b97ea6ddf4ed047cc851740c3854be5650eb9e76df7f5a3d1456f8" :answer/id #uuid "193a2a0c-115e-5019-b79d-76d12bee3379" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I  only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64258/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "f47cdecf-f0ee-5e1b-adaf-413e74927b66" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64258/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "f68ec0ef-035e-5abd-bf81-720c3c073497" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64258/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "be5dc12e-937a-5396-b673-5b5d414d4041" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-64258/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-64258/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-64258/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-64258/selection/18f1ea50d5b97ea6ddf4ed047cc851740c3854be5650eb9e76df7f5a3d1456f8" "q-64258/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-64258/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-64258" :question/id #uuid "159cb1ab-7613-5b55-bdef-c9e84b2bbb54" :question/math-academy-id "q-64258" :question/problem "A particle of mass $32\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0$\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{4}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{4}$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{8}{32}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}$ in form 1 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-64258/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-64258"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94285/selection/b93e53501cd2fc582b14c41183633e0325dcfb639ed84ddfda748648cf5d0fc2" :answer/id #uuid "18b44069-623b-5305-962f-1acaecf4a697" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+8y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94285/selection/e1980b9aacd96dc4c8f4f27b1698d762a6b8a1e8c4a14ab0260ab895119d943e" :answer/id #uuid "9886a6b3-db66-5c66-8a3a-3a8b025d5f77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94285/selection/a5b1b6149e4280494257bf7203e3885812e833e63f04ce1f9221093da67be0c9" :answer/id #uuid "015c5e06-90a8-5651-a527-1d484e92abb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94285/selection/294fa4c918c9b7876fbefbcd88070c8275c1a70b4df236e1cd2c2d52f40ff71f" :answer/id #uuid "6be1dbf9-bc53-5c6f-9ddf-e4bc9ba17646" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94285/selection/2050771de2ea94095720b9cdfbc82a2699370003871d659a8a4f9d44b1efa388" :answer/id #uuid "e412fc93-9fe1-5f1d-8399-9d3eb682e3fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94285/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f55f56a-ce6d-57ff-99cf-ffdc5fdb0639" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94285/selection/2050771de2ea94095720b9cdfbc82a2699370003871d659a8a4f9d44b1efa388" :answer-field/choices ["q-94285/selection/b93e53501cd2fc582b14c41183633e0325dcfb639ed84ddfda748648cf5d0fc2" "q-94285/selection/e1980b9aacd96dc4c8f4f27b1698d762a6b8a1e8c4a14ab0260ab895119d943e" "q-94285/selection/a5b1b6149e4280494257bf7203e3885812e833e63f04ce1f9221093da67be0c9" "q-94285/selection/294fa4c918c9b7876fbefbcd88070c8275c1a70b4df236e1cd2c2d52f40ff71f" "q-94285/selection/2050771de2ea94095720b9cdfbc82a2699370003871d659a8a4f9d44b1efa388"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94285" :question/id #uuid "c79644b9-1bd1-5d4c-a08e-a12fa9aff392" :question/math-academy-id "q-94285" :question/problem "A particle of mass $16\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=4\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that:\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{4}{16}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94285/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94285"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-64255/selection/540e313dce431a8ea3a1de5db209ef5f43385502c2cd8783650d122cbe365603" :answer/id #uuid "1e937a2f-6b2f-5d0e-ba64-cd4bab91487e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}-4y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64255/selection/6dbacc9d60a2a7eaf045ddab96602ed681aae4aaf129a04f4a5984c9b222f9ab" :answer/id #uuid "79e7f7fd-2ece-5807-8865-a9add8e72f3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{2}y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64255/selection/a5b1b6149e4280494257bf7203e3885812e833e63f04ce1f9221093da67be0c9" :answer/id #uuid "0aa56111-5e00-5839-99bf-f8f70c82ed0b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64255/selection/e1980b9aacd96dc4c8f4f27b1698d762a6b8a1e8c4a14ab0260ab895119d943e" :answer/id #uuid "f5fc45a7-8311-5d8e-aa58-c9605249b0b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64255/selection/294fa4c918c9b7876fbefbcd88070c8275c1a70b4df236e1cd2c2d52f40ff71f" :answer/id #uuid "532f318f-ffb1-56ce-a0c7-d7d7e51f14eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-64255/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "544c537f-d29d-559f-97f9-d59051c19179" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-64255/selection/e1980b9aacd96dc4c8f4f27b1698d762a6b8a1e8c4a14ab0260ab895119d943e" :answer-field/choices ["q-64255/selection/540e313dce431a8ea3a1de5db209ef5f43385502c2cd8783650d122cbe365603" "q-64255/selection/6dbacc9d60a2a7eaf045ddab96602ed681aae4aaf129a04f4a5984c9b222f9ab" "q-64255/selection/a5b1b6149e4280494257bf7203e3885812e833e63f04ce1f9221093da67be0c9" "q-64255/selection/e1980b9aacd96dc4c8f4f27b1698d762a6b8a1e8c4a14ab0260ab895119d943e" "q-64255/selection/294fa4c918c9b7876fbefbcd88070c8275c1a70b4df236e1cd2c2d52f40ff71f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-64255" :question/id #uuid "b407710d-82b6-5413-b053-42b11d925365" :question/math-academy-id "q-64255" :question/problem "A particle of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=32\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{32}{8}=4.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-64255/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-64255"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-64229"] :question/problem "A particle of mass $12\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=24\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{24}{12}=2.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6619"] :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?\n\n- $y'' + 81y = 0$\n\n- $y'' + 9y'+81y = 0$\n\n- $y'' - 81y = 0$" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- Equation I takes the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 81 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\omega = 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I represents SHM.\n\n- Equation II does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nbecause it has a $y'$ term.\n\n- Equation III does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt would require $\\omega^2 = -81,$ which is not possible for any real number $\\omega$ since $\\omega^2 \\geq 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6620"] :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 100y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere the period $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 100\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 10,\n$$\n\nso the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{10} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\,\\textrm{s}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6621"] :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 9 y = 0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y = R\\cos(3t+\\phi_1)$\n\n- $y = R\\sin(9t+\\phi_2)$\n\n- $y = A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3t}$\n\nNote that $R,\\phi_1,\\phi_2, A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2y = 0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n- $y = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n- $y =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 9\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\omega=3$ into the three different forms of the general solution gives the following:\n\n- $y = R\\cos{(3t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n- $y = R\\sin{(3t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n- $y =A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3 t}$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6622"] :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 36y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $3\\,\\textrm{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0,\n$$\n\nand the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi)},\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 36\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 6,\n$$\n\nso the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(6t+\\phi)}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0) = 0.$ Substituting this into the equation above gives\n\n$$\nR\\sin\\phi = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\phi = 0.\n$$\n\nSince $\\phi=0,$ our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{6 t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $3\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that $y'(0) = 3.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = 6R\\cos{6t},\n$$\n\nand substituting $y'(0) = 3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3 = 6R\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad R=\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\textrm{m}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6623"] :question/problem "A particle of mass $36\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is $y'' + \\dfrac{1}{2}y = 0$\n\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi}\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}$\n\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)},$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m=36$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k=9$ has\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = \\dfrac{k}{m} = \\dfrac{9}{36} = \\dfrac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nso its equation of motion is \n\n$$\ny'' + \\dfrac{1}{4}y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich is different from the equation in statement I.\n\n- Statement II is true. The frequency $f$ (measured in $s^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf = \\dfrac{\\omega}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi} \\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\\,R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1}),\\,R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $\\omega = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\\phi_1 = \\phi$ in the form $R\\sin{(\\omega t+\\phi_2)}$ gives\n\n$$\ny(t) = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
